In `DecodeDirect<Uni16>::decode`:
```cpp
void decode(const char * in0, int size, FilterCharVector & out) const {
    const Chr * in = reinterpret_cast<const Chr *>(in0);
    if (size == -1) {
        for (;*in; ++in)
            out.append(*in);
    } else {
        const Chr * stop = reinterpret_cast<const Chr *>(in0 +size);
        for (;in != stop; ++in)
            out.append(*in);
    }
}
```

When `Chr` is `Uni16` (2 bytes), and `size` is the number of bytes, `in0 + size` points to `size` bytes past the start. Then `reinterpret_cast<const Chr *>(in0 + size)` treats that as a pointer to `Uni16`. The loop iterates while `in != stop`, reading 2 bytes each time.

If `size` is odd, `in0 + size` is not aligned to a 2-byte boundary, and the loop will read 2 bytes at a time, potentially reading 1 byte past the end of the buffer.

